## [ISSUE] Transient failures now permanently delete reminders after two cron runs (~30 min)

**Severity:** HIGH — a short Twilio/network outage or a rate-limit window silently destroys reminders that were days away from being due.

### The Problem

```ts
} catch (error) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({ where: { id: reminder.id }, data: { retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1 } });
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
}
```

and at the top of the handler:

```ts
{ retryCount: { gt: 1 } }   // -> deleteMany
```

Every exception is treated as a failed attempt, regardless of whether it is permanent (invalid number, Twilio error 21211) or transient.

### Theory / Proof

- `.github/workflows/cron-scheduleSMSReminders.yml` runs the job `*/15 * * * *`. Two failed runs -> `retryCount = 2` -> deleted on the third run. That is a 30-45 minute window.
- The findMany selects reminders up to **7 days** ahead (`scheduledDate lte now+7d`), so the reminder being deleted may not be due for days; previously it would have been retried ~670 times before its date.
- Throws that reach this catch include, from `twilioProvider.scheduleSMS` (`packages/features/ee/workflows/lib/reminders/providers/twilioProvider.ts:79-114`): `createTwilioClient()` throwing on missing env, `checkSMSRateLimit({ rateLimitingType: "smsMonth" })` throwing when a user exceeds 250 SMS / 30d (`packages/lib/rateLimit.ts:91-96`), and any `twilio.messages.create` rejection (HTTP 429/5xx, network timeouts). Also any exception in template rendering or the `prisma.profile.findFirst` call above.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Before this PR, a transient failure simply meant the reminder was picked up on the next run. After this PR, a 30-minute Twilio incident, a misconfigured deployment env, or a user hitting the monthly SMS cap near a month boundary deletes every pending SMS reminder in the 7-day window, with no log beyond a `console.log` and no way to recover.

### Research

Twilio documents which errors are permanent (e.g. 21211 invalid 'To' number, 21614 not a mobile number) vs. retryable (HTTP 429, 5xx); the Twilio Node SDK surfaces these as `RestException` with `.status` and `.code`. Standard retry guidance (AWS Builders' Library, "Timeouts, retries and backoff") is to retry only on retryable errors and to budget retries by time, not by a fixed small count on a 15-minute schedule.

### Recommendation

Only count errors that are known to be permanent, and let everything else retry until `scheduledDate` as before:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  const isPermanent =
    error instanceof RestException && error.status >= 400 && error.status < 500 && error.status !== 429;
  if (isPermanent) {
    await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
      where: { id: reminder.id },
      data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } },
    });
  }
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
}
```

## [ISSUE] SMS-locked users/teams have all their reminders deleted via the new `else` branch

**Severity:** HIGH — an admin SMS lock, which is meant to be a reversible hold, now irreversibly destroys the account's pending reminders.

### The Problem

```ts
if (scheduledSMS) {
  ...scheduled: true...
} else {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({ ..., data: { retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1 } });
}
```

The only path in `twilio.scheduleSMS` that resolves to a falsy value is the lock check (`packages/features/ee/workflows/lib/reminders/providers/twilioProvider.ts:90-95`):

```ts
const isSMSSendingLocked = await isLockedForSMSSending(userId, teamId);
if (isSMSSendingLocked) {
  log.debug(...is locked for SMS sending);
  return;
}
```

`twilio.messages.create` otherwise either resolves with a `MessageInstance` or throws, so the `else` branch is effectively "account is SMS-locked", not "sending failed".

### Theory / Proof

`isLockedForSMSSending` (`twilioProvider.ts:148-189`) returns true when the team or user, or any team the user is a member of, has `smsLockState === LOCKED`. That state is set/unset by admins (`SMSLockState` enum: LOCKED / UNLOCKED / REVIEW_NEEDED). With the cron at `*/15`, two runs while locked set `retryCount = 2`, and the `OR: [{ retryCount: { gt: 1 } }]` branch of `deleteMany` deletes them.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Before: reminders of a locked account stayed `scheduled: false` and were scheduled automatically once an admin unlocked the account. After: 30 minutes after a lock, every pending SMS reminder for that user (and, via memberships, for every member of a locked team) is deleted; unlocking no longer restores anything. The PR description targets invalid phone numbers, which surface as Twilio exceptions, not as this branch.

### Research

The same lock path in `sendSMS` is used as a silent no-op elsewhere in the codebase; callers treat `undefined` as "skipped", not as an error.

### Recommendation

Do not count the lock as a failed attempt. Either drop the `else` branch, or have `scheduleSMS` report why it returned nothing:

```ts
if (scheduledSMS) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
    where: { id: reminder.id },
    data: { scheduled: true, referenceId: scheduledSMS.sid },
  });
}
// no else: undefined means the account is SMS-locked; keep the reminder for when it is unlocked
```

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Reminders with no phone number or empty message are still retried forever

**Severity:** LOW — wasted cron work every 15 minutes for reminders that can never succeed.

### The Problem

```ts
if (message?.length && message?.length > 0 && sendTo) {
  ...
  if (scheduledSMS) { ... } else { retryCount + 1 }
}
// no else: nothing recorded
```

If `sendTo` is null (e.g. `SMS_ATTENDEE` where `booking.smsReminderNumber` was never collected, or `SMS_NUMBER` with no `sendTo`) or the template yields no message (non-REMINDER template with empty `reminderBody`), the reminder falls through without touching `retryCount`. Similarly `if (!reminder.workflowStep || !reminder.booking) continue;` at line 68.

### What happens today vs. what will break

These are deterministic, permanent conditions: the next run will make the same decision. They keep being selected by `findMany` and re-evaluated every 15 minutes (including the `prisma.profile.findFirst` query at line 111) until `scheduledDate` passes. The PR's stated goal is to stop exactly this repeated scheduling for reminders that cannot be sent.

### Recommendation

Count (or immediately delete) these reminders too:

```ts
if (message?.length && sendTo) {
  ...
} else {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
    where: { id: reminder.id },
    data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } },
  });
}
```

## [ISSUE] Unguarded `update` inside `catch` can abort the whole batch

**Severity:** LOW — one bad row can stop scheduling for every reminder after it in the run.

### The Problem

```ts
} catch (error) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({ where: { id: reminder.id }, data: { retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1 } });
  console.log(...);
}
```

Previously the catch only logged, so one failing reminder could never stop the `for` loop. Now the catch performs I/O that can itself throw, and nothing catches it.

### Theory / Proof

`prisma.x.update` throws `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` P2025 when the row does not exist. Reminders are deleted concurrently by `handleCancelBooking.ts`, `requestReschedule.handler.ts` and workflow update/delete handlers while this cron iterates. If the original error was itself a database error (connection pool exhausted, timeout), the update in the catch will likely fail the same way. Either case rejects `handler`, `defaultHandler` returns 500, and every remaining reminder in `unscheduledReminders` is skipped for this run.

### Recommendation

Use an atomic increment and do not let bookkeeping failures escape:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
  await prisma.workflowReminder
    .update({ where: { id: reminder.id }, data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } } })
    .catch((e) => console.log(`Failed to update retryCount: ${e}`));
}
```
